# Decide how to treat checks that do not apply: auth, A2A, WebMCP, DNS-AID, commerce

## Step

The [checks asset](./research-agent-ready-checks-asset.svx) settles the gate: Level 5 is Level 4 plus two of three groups, and this site honestly clears none of them as planned. The groups: (a) Web Bot Auth, a JWKS at `/.well-known/http-message-signatures-directory` whose private key signs the site's own outbound bot requests; (b) all four integrations, which adds an A2A agent card that is only truthful if `/a2a` implements A2A JSON-RPC; (c) auth metadata, OAuth discovery or an `auth.md` that names real registration endpoints.

Decide which two groups the site earns rather than fakes. Recommendation: (b) by implementing a minimal real A2A endpoint at `/a2a` whose one skill answers questions from the same knowledge base the MCP serves (A2A is JSON-RPC over HTTP, small enough for one Worker route), and (a) only if the site ever sends requests; otherwise (c) with an honest `auth.md` that says access is anonymous and documents no registration, if the scanner's parser accepts it (unverified; test against the live scanner before relying on it). If neither second group is honest, the finish line drops to "Level 4 plus every applicable check" and the brief says why. Commerce stays out of scope; WebMCP stays out because the root is not an HTML app.
